00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.950
And we will leave a link, by
the way, in the description.

00:00:54.075 --> 00:00:54.495
Awesome.

00:00:54.525 --> 00:01:00.375
Uh, yeah, I just mainly talk about my
experiences that I have as a German living

00:01:00.375 --> 00:01:02.175
in the US in the Midwest specifically.

00:01:02.175 --> 00:01:03.705
I live in Cincinnati, Ohio.

00:01:04.095 --> 00:01:08.205
Um, and I kind of ended up
here by accident, if you will.

00:01:08.414 --> 00:01:10.875
I did an exchange semester
during university.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.375
I am from Munich.

00:01:12.375 --> 00:01:16.305
That's also where I went to university,
and that was like during the last

00:01:16.305 --> 00:01:17.685
semester of my bachelor's degree.

00:01:17.685 --> 00:01:21.885
And then I just really liked my
time in Cincinnati and I kinda

00:01:21.975 --> 00:01:23.355
got stuck here, if you will.

00:01:23.715 --> 00:01:27.045
And obviously that was a
little over six years ago.

00:01:27.375 --> 00:01:33.735
Since then, I've encountered a lot of
things that have made my life better or

00:01:33.735 --> 00:01:36.945
more challenging living in the US as a
German, you know, things that you would

00:01:36.945 --> 00:01:40.881
never think about, like how suddenly
getting groceries is a challenge 'cause

00:01:40.881 --> 00:01:44.070
you don't know how certain things work
'casue like, You're just so used to

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.500
how things work in your home country
and suddenly you're here and you

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.830
have to deal with all these everyday
things and you're like, wait, how?

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.310
Like, I remember one of the first things
that was really embarrassing to me

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:58.905
was that the, the whipped cream that
comes out of the spray can, those spray

00:01:58.905 --> 00:02:00.375
cans are differently for some reason.

00:02:00.495 --> 00:02:04.455
And I was like with my roommates,
I was 22 years old and I had to

00:02:04.455 --> 00:02:07.365
ask them how to work this thing and
they were like, wait, you've never

00:02:07.365 --> 00:02:08.985
like used one of these spray cans?

00:02:08.985 --> 00:02:11.175
I was like, yeah, have, but
they're different in Germany.

00:02:11.175 --> 00:02:12.825
I don't know how to, how to work this one.

00:02:13.185 --> 00:02:16.995
And that's kind of like overall, what I
talk on my channel is just these little

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:21.540
difficulties and things that I really,
really enjoy about my life in the US,

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.400
and then I also sometimes try to teach my
American audience about German culture,

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:33.630
German holidays, Octoberfest, um, things
that people in the US ask me about a lot

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.010
whenever they find out that I'm German.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.680
You know, it's interesting of course,
whatever you find different in the US as

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.940
a German, uh, Americans are gonna find
different in Germany when they're there.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.040
So yes, it kind of goes both ways.

00:02:47.385 --> 00:02:47.875
Yeah.

00:02:47.875 --> 00:02:53.085
But, uh, yeah, and of course talking
about the things that are interesting

00:02:53.085 --> 00:02:58.455
or different or fun in Germany can be
a motivator for people to learn German,

00:02:58.935 --> 00:03:03.435
which, uh, you know, is a major language,
major language in Europe, major language

00:03:03.435 --> 00:03:04.716
in history, and so forth and so on.

00:03:04.995 --> 00:03:08.025
But what are some of the
things, so you arrive as a...

00:03:08.455 --> 00:03:09.959
well, were you 22?

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.690
Did, did I hear you say you were
22 when you arrived in Cincinnati?

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:13.049
Yeah.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:13.739
That's right.

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:16.589
I, so I wonder if you were
to pick sort of, okay...

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.500
how, how about you pick three
or five things that you found

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:24.209
agreeably different in the US and
three or five things that you found

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:26.310
disagreeably different in the US?

00:03:28.019 --> 00:03:29.609
I actually have to ask,
what does that mean?

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:31.739
Agreeably different and
disagreeably different?

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:35.519
Well, uh, this is different than at home.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:36.545
This is better.

00:03:37.525 --> 00:03:38.385
This is more fun.

00:03:38.385 --> 00:03:39.345
This is more pleasant.

00:03:39.705 --> 00:03:41.895
Or these are things that are different.

00:03:41.925 --> 00:03:43.425
Gee, it was better at home.

00:03:44.025 --> 00:03:44.745
Okay, gotcha.

00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:50.355
Um, so I would say the things that
I really liked about the US that I

00:03:50.385 --> 00:03:55.905
still like better about the US is the
mentality, but not all of the mentality.

00:03:55.905 --> 00:03:58.455
Of course, there's certain things that
I like more about German mentality,

00:03:58.695 --> 00:04:05.880
but for me at the time, I really
noticed how the more positive attitude

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:10.350
towards a lot of things really
influenced me in a positive way.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:14.130
Like I felt like from a personal
development, it was great being in an

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:18.209
environment where when I told people
that I had a plan to do something,

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:22.050
um, and you know, like a, an idea
for, to start a YouTube channel,

00:04:22.055 --> 00:04:25.250
for example, people would usually
just go, oh my God, that's so cool.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:26.280
Go for it.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:27.000
I love that.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:27.840
That's awesome.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:33.525
Whereas in Germany, my environment
was more typical German in that sense,

00:04:33.525 --> 00:04:35.985
where it would usually be like, oh, why?

00:04:36.045 --> 00:04:37.125
Are you sure about that?

00:04:37.425 --> 00:04:42.675
Um, and I like to summarize that
with, in my experience, Germans, if

00:04:42.675 --> 00:04:46.995
you wanna summarize it, have more
of this like, approach to life and

00:04:46.995 --> 00:04:50.835
a lot of other decisions in, in, in
life uh, where it's more like, why?

00:04:51.735 --> 00:04:53.530
And Americans are always
more like, why not?

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:55.960
Like, just try it and
what do you have to lose?

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.525
And that was like the main thing that I
really, really liked about being here.

00:04:59.755 --> 00:05:02.530
Because I just felt a lot
more encouraged to do things.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:05.290
Um, honestly, that's the main thing.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:08.830
Other than that I would probably
have to say, and it's kind of kind

00:05:08.835 --> 00:05:13.680
of embarrassing that I really started
to like the convenience in the US and

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.110
that's not necessarily something I'm
proud of, but it was kind of fun to just

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:22.710
be able to do anything you want at any
time and you didn't really have to plan.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.800
Like I just really like being
spontaneous in this country.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:29.430
You can go to the store at 1:00 AM
if you wanna buy something great,

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.970
you don't have to plan ahead because
stores are close on Sundays, et cetera.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:37.725
So, and also, I found to be much
more spontaneous when I wanted to do

00:05:37.725 --> 00:05:41.595
something at night with friends, I
could just hit up like 10 of my friends

00:05:41.595 --> 00:05:44.925
and at least half of them would be
free and we would just do something.

00:05:44.925 --> 00:05:48.105
Whereas in Germany, you sometimes have
to ask people like two weeks in advance.

00:05:49.125 --> 00:05:51.285
Germans are not like as
spontaneous in general.

00:05:51.765 --> 00:05:53.535
Um, let me think.

00:05:53.535 --> 00:05:54.015
Is there like...

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:57.969
I would say probably the third thing,
'cause those are like the main things.

00:05:57.969 --> 00:06:01.660
I think my main points were
always just people's mentality.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:06.610
'casue when it comes to the system of
the country, there are more things that

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:11.020
I criticize, I think about it than I
actually prefer over the German system in

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.420
terms of, you know, healthcare, et cetera.

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:15.310
Just the, the typical topics.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:21.645
But the third one would probably
be how things are less complicated

00:06:21.645 --> 00:06:23.305
in terms of bureaucracy in the US.

00:06:23.675 --> 00:06:27.345
So for me, like buying a, I
bought a house here for example,

00:06:27.495 --> 00:06:28.785
that was super quick and easy.

00:06:28.785 --> 00:06:30.075
I was kind of shocked by it.

00:06:30.375 --> 00:06:32.025
Um, I started a business here.

00:06:32.025 --> 00:06:36.105
All those things were just really
easy for me and I felt like even

00:06:36.105 --> 00:06:38.955
though I was new to the country and
to the system, I could tackle them.

00:06:39.615 --> 00:06:42.645
So those would be my three
things that I like better.

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:47.625
In terms of things that I don't like
as much, I actually made a video

00:06:47.625 --> 00:06:48.885
about that at the very beginning.

00:06:49.155 --> 00:06:51.075
One of the things was washing machines.

00:06:51.345 --> 00:06:55.185
That's probably way too,
uh, particular of a topic.

00:06:55.185 --> 00:06:59.085
But, um, when I first came here, American
washing machines really drove me crazy.

00:06:59.604 --> 00:07:05.670
Um but I would say the general things, I
like the German healthcare system better.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.450
Um, you don't have to pay for an
ambulance, you don't have to worry

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:10.920
if you go to the emergency room.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.530
Um, you know, those kinds of things.

00:07:13.535 --> 00:07:18.930
Doctors don't cost tens of thousands of
dollars just for taking a look at you.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:22.950
Um, I had surgery here actually one
time, and I saw the hospital bill.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:24.000
It was a risk surgery.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.380
It took like four hours and the hospital
bill said $50,000 just for the surgery.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:32.730
Um, so that's definitely something
I like better about Germany.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:34.710
Same as the education system.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:36.900
I like a lot about the
American education system.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:41.370
I love campus life and all the
stuff that's included, but it's

00:07:41.375 --> 00:07:43.890
just not accessible to people.

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:48.745
It's just so insanely expensive in
so many students in the US graduate

00:07:48.745 --> 00:07:53.460
college with a hundred thousand
dollars debt, which is normal.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:56.050
And if you go to a better college,
you have way more than that.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:59.590
So that's definitely something
I like better in Germany.

00:08:00.735 --> 00:08:06.105
And in some ways I also have to
contradict myself to what I said earlier.

00:08:06.255 --> 00:08:08.235
In some ways I like the
German mentality better.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:12.675
So for example, when it comes to
directness, I sometimes really miss

00:08:12.705 --> 00:08:17.965
in the US that I don't know exactly
where I stand with a person 'cause

00:08:18.075 --> 00:08:20.325
they're gonna be overly friendly.

00:08:20.325 --> 00:08:23.205
Um, when it comes to business
also, it's sometimes difficult.

00:08:23.415 --> 00:08:25.735
You can't just be direct with them.

00:08:25.735 --> 00:08:29.395
If someone does a bad job, I feel like I
have to kind of tiptoe around it first.

00:08:29.755 --> 00:08:32.335
Um, so sometimes I really
miss the German directness.

00:08:32.335 --> 00:08:32.465
Okay.

00:08:33.605 --> 00:08:36.479
Well that's very, very...

00:08:36.479 --> 00:08:37.579
That was a long answer, I know.

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:38.900
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:39.630
No, very interesting.

00:08:40.229 --> 00:08:43.500
Uh, so, and as someone, you
know, I, I lived in Japan.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:45.060
I lived in France for three years.

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:49.140
It went back to Canada, and Canada's quite
similar to the US in many, many ways.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:52.829
And it is true that it's easier
to do things, uh, you know, just

00:08:52.829 --> 00:08:54.540
getting a bank loan or, or anything.

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:55.380
It's just easier.

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:59.615
People are more inclined to just make
it easy to do things, whereas in the

00:08:59.615 --> 00:09:03.155
older countries, they're inclined to
make it more complicated to do things.

00:09:03.695 --> 00:09:07.565
Uh, Canada's a little more bureaucratic
than the US and I think that's why

00:09:07.565 --> 00:09:10.745
in the US there's such a dynamism.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:12.275
You know, they're business dynamism.

00:09:12.275 --> 00:09:14.705
They're either go for it, they
do things and the regulations

00:09:14.705 --> 00:09:15.605
don't stand in the way.

00:09:16.115 --> 00:09:17.735
And, and people are very friendly.

00:09:17.735 --> 00:09:22.275
I found people in Germany uh,
insofar as you know, healthcare

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:23.745
Canada's kind of in between Germany.

00:09:23.745 --> 00:09:26.865
Canada has a universal coverage
system, but it's not as

00:09:26.865 --> 00:09:28.125
good as the one in Germany.

00:09:28.485 --> 00:09:31.615
Uh, fewer beds per
capita, fewer, you know...

00:09:31.755 --> 00:09:36.975
and a little more ideological sort
of rigidity to not allow any kind

00:09:36.975 --> 00:09:40.875
of private delivery of services or
strictly limit private delivery.

00:09:41.155 --> 00:09:46.285
So the system's kind of quite fragile, but
I think I agree with you, the principle

00:09:46.285 --> 00:09:51.505
of universal coverage is, is I, I can't
see any argument for not having universal

00:09:51.505 --> 00:09:54.325
coverage and uh, similarly with education.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:57.085
It is, I agree with you, it's
ridiculously expensive in the us.

00:09:57.550 --> 00:10:00.700
Um, so, but those are
things that can be altered.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:04.510
On the other hand, there is a dynamism
in the US which is undeniable,

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:08.020
more dynamic, uh, commercially
business-wise than Canada, more

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:11.890
dynamic than Germany, although the
Germans absolutely are more thorough.

00:10:12.189 --> 00:10:15.580
So all of our appliances at
home are at German appliances.

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:23.350
They're thorough, you know,
and they're gonna make it.

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:29.880
Whereas, uh, north America, It's,
it's kind of, you know, whatever.

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:30.810
This is good enough.

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:31.950
So out the door it goes.

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:34.470
I'm exaggerating, but it
was quite interesting.

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:36.990
Your observations would
be quite similar to mine.

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:44.130
Um, but by and large, one other thing,
you know, so you're living in the US

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:50.475
um, when we go to another country, It's
almost unreal because we're living in a

00:10:50.805 --> 00:10:54.795
very different environment, even though
culturally it's not such a big difference.

00:10:54.795 --> 00:10:57.495
It's not like going to Japan,
but when you're living in another

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:00.015
culture, it's every day is an
adventure because it's a di...

00:11:00.045 --> 00:11:04.125
so it's fun and, and there are, you can
focus in on the things that you don't

00:11:04.125 --> 00:11:08.565
like and be unhappy, or you can focus in
on the things that you like and enjoy it.

00:11:08.865 --> 00:11:12.300
So the time I lived in Japan, there
were things that annoyed me in

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:14.160
Japan, but I didn't focus in on that.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:17.370
I focused in on the things that
I like and, and I think that's

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:19.020
important in learning languages.

00:11:19.025 --> 00:11:22.260
Focus in on things that you like, 'cause
if you like the language and you like

00:11:22.265 --> 00:11:24.990
the people and you like the culture,
you're gonna learn the language.

00:11:25.635 --> 00:11:26.985
So you obviously...

00:11:26.985 --> 00:11:27.625
a lot of people...

00:11:28.225 --> 00:11:28.575
go ahead.

00:11:28.665 --> 00:11:31.605
I was gonna say, a lot of people also
like find their approach through music.

00:11:31.605 --> 00:11:34.545
For example, they love German
music., Rammsteinis a big one,

00:11:34.545 --> 00:11:39.075
for example, for a lot of people,
but also some were hip hop music.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:39.795
German music.

00:11:40.065 --> 00:11:44.555
Um, I know it was a great mo motivator
for a lot of people to learn German.

00:11:45.475 --> 00:11:45.795
Yeah.

00:11:45.855 --> 00:11:48.045
But try to be positive
because you can be selective.

00:11:48.435 --> 00:11:51.765
You don't have to focus in, oh,
someone, you know, I went to the store

00:11:51.765 --> 00:11:54.825
and I tried to speak German and they
came, they answered in English and

00:11:54.825 --> 00:11:56.625
now I'm, I'm giving up on German.

00:11:56.625 --> 00:12:00.165
'cause person A replied in
English, don't focus in on that.

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:02.175
Go find someone who's gonna
talk to you in German.

00:12:02.175 --> 00:12:04.755
You know, it's important to be positive.

00:12:04.814 --> 00:12:08.655
And you obviously, despite your
observations, which might be

00:12:08.655 --> 00:12:11.995
at times a little critical,
overwhelmingly you are very positive

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:13.635
about your experience in the US.

00:12:13.895 --> 00:12:15.705
And I think that's the way one has to be.

00:12:15.705 --> 00:12:19.635
I'm very positive about my experience
when I come to winter in Palm Springs.

00:12:19.785 --> 00:12:22.815
We had a party last night with our
neighbors, all Americans, actually.

00:12:22.815 --> 00:12:25.575
There's one Canadian, and,
and it's, it's, you can be

00:12:26.045 --> 00:12:27.225
positive about wherever you are.

00:12:27.225 --> 00:12:30.855
You can find people that you like, find
things in the culture that you like.

00:12:30.855 --> 00:12:32.595
And I, I think it's
important to stay positive.

